hoping to tackle the causes of knife  crime at a grassroots level this  community football game is being played  to raise awareness in the community  about knife crime  and to remember its victims  including 16 year old damari roy who was  murdered in a knife attack last july  he was a keen footballer  his dad dwayne says these games are  important to bring people in the  community together and keep children  safe  he has two sons who often have to pass  the spot where their brother was killed  they go through the door you don't know  if they're going to come back that's the  maori didn't we're doing our best in in  the community to be honest we're trying  we're trying our best to get the use  them off the street but we need help  the governments have to help us to do  that because we can't do it on our own  last year there were nearly 47 000  serious offences involving a knife in  england and wales according to the most  recent ons statistics  and according to victim support's  national homicide service 77 percent of  young people who were murdered were  killed with a knife  everyone agrees more needs to be done to  stop young people being killed like this  but not a consensus about how to achieve  that do you put more money into  community outreach projects and youth  centres police patrols what about stop  and search on monday pretty patel  reduced restrictions on those  controversial police powers  dwayne supports stop research as an  important crime prevention tool but  there are not many others here who  welcome it  damari's friend alex is playing today to  remember him and encourage the community  to do more  let's teach them right from wrong let's  make them aware of what's on the street  also more can be done in schools with  workshops there should be more workshops  people going into schools educating  children on the dangers of carrying a  knife days like this are one way to  celebrate the memories of those lost to  knife crime and are important for the  community but everyone agrees it will  take more to bring about lasting change  madeleine ratcliffe sky news
 

